PART 4 -- where that mass lives, which is what decides whether any lensing statistic can see it
======================================================================================================================
    M_b [Msun]  r_M [kpc]     r_EFE [kpc] (no clu / max clu)  r_EFE [Mpc] max
         1e+09        1.2               55 /             19            0.055
         1e+10        3.9              174 /             60            0.174
         1e+11       12.2              551 /            188            0.551
         3e+11       21.1              955 /            326            0.955
  [PASS] 4a the phantom is a SMALL-SCALE component: even the most massive discs carry it out to at most a few hundred kiloparsecs, so it lives entirely in the one-halo regime   (largest r_EFE in the table = 0.95 Mpc (a 3e11 Msun disc in the weakest measured field))
  [PASS] 4b and that fixes the wavenumbers at which it can appear at all.  CMB lensing peaks near L ~ 40, which is sourced by k ~ 0.01-0.1 h/Mpc at z ~ 2; cosmic-shear S8 is measured at k ~ 0.1-1.  The phantom starts at k > 2pi/r_EFE, far above both   (the phantom only contributes above k ~ 7 h/Mpc, against k ~ 0.03 (CMB lensing) and k ~ 0.1-1 (S8))

  THE SHARPEST USE OF THE SAME CLOSED FORM, and it is a test rather than a budget: for a single galaxy the
  framework PREDICTS the total dynamical mass, M_dyn = nu(g_ext/a_0) M_bar, with nothing fitted.  For an L*
  disc that number can be compared with the halo mass the stellar-to-halo-mass relation measures.
       bracket    footing   nu(e_N)   M_dyn predicted   M_h measured (SHMR)   ratio
    no cluster  canonical      45.7          2.74e+12              1.50e+12    1.83
    no cluster        alt      50.1          3.01e+12              1.50e+12    2.01
   max cluster  canonical      16.0          9.57e+11              1.50e+12    0.64
   max cluster        alt      17.5          1.05e+12              1.50e+12    0.70
  [PASS] 4c AND THE MEASURED HALO MASS OF AN L* GALAXY SITS INSIDE THE TWO BRACKETS -- which is a real, if blunt, pass for a zero-parameter prediction, and simultaneously says that the external field, not a_0, is what needs measuring next   (M_dyn(predicted)/M_h(measured) = 0.64 (strongest field) to 2.01 (weakest field); the measurement is bracketed, and pinning g_ext to 0.3 dex would turn this into a test)

======================================================================================================================
PART 5 -- the two anomalies, answered with numbers rather than with adjectives
======================================================================================================================
     Planck 2018 lensing amplitude A_L = 1.180 +- 0.065 (TT,TE,EE+lowE), i.e. an 18% excess to explain.
     The ABSOLUTE UPPER BOUND the framework can supply is the whole phantom treated as if it lensed the CMB
     as efficiently as smooth matter: delta A_L <= 2 x Omega_ph/Omega_m = 0.853.
     The real number is far smaller, because A_L is sourced at k ~ 0.03 h/Mpc where the phantom is absent.
  [PASS] 5a REPORTED AGAINST MY OWN FIRST DRAFT: the crude MASS bound does NOT dispose of the A_L anomaly -- the phantom carries enough mass in principle.  What disposes of it is the SCALE, and only the scale: the CMB lensing power at L ~ 40 is sourced near k ~ 0.03 h/Mpc, while the phantom lives above k ~ 7 h/Mpc, more than two decades away, where the CMB lensing kernel has essentially no weight.  A proper calculation is a halo-model integral this script does not do, and saying so is part of the result   (mass bound delta A_L <= 0.85 (larger than the 0.18 excess, so uninformative); scale separation k_phantom/k_A_L = 219, which is what actually settles it)
  
     S8: weak lensing measures S8 ~ 0.76, the CMB predicts 0.83 -- lensing sees LESS structure than expected.
     The framework ADDS mass (43% of Omega_m at most, on sub-Mpc scales), so it moves the
     lensing amplitude UP.  Its sign is therefore wrong for the S8 tension: it makes it worse, not better.
  [PASS] 5b THE S8 TENSION IS THE WRONG SIGN FOR THE FRAMEWORK, and that is a liability rather than a hit.  The phantom is extra mass on top of a dark sector the CMB has already fixed, so any lensing statistic it touches moves AWAY from the low weak-lensing amplitude   (the framework adds up to 42.7% to Omega_m as seen by small-scale lensing, and the S8 tension needs a REDUCTION of about 8% in the amplitude)
  
     The one place the number is useful is the framework's OWN double-counting fork (items 84, 73): if the
     dark sector also virialises into halos, the framework has Omega_dm halos PLUS this phantom on top.
     The phantom alone is 51% of Omega_dm, so that branch over-predicts small-scale
     galaxy-galaxy lensing by that much -- a testable, non-negligible cost of the virialising branch.

======================================================================================================================
PART 6 -- mutation control and the Upsilon lever, computed rather than asserted
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] 6a MUTATION: with a_0 driven to zero, e_N = g_ext/a_0 diverges, the kernel becomes the identity and the phantom must vanish exactly.  (The first version of this check divided by a HUGE a_0 instead of a tiny one and drove nu the wrong way -- it returned 2.6e3 rather than 0, and the check caught it.)   (Omega_phantom(a_0 -> 0) = 0.000e+00)
  [PASS] 6b THE UPSILON LEVER, and it is large: the stellar mass density is an SED-fitted quantity, so three quarters of Omega_bar,gal moves with the assumed stellar mass-to-light ratio.  The phantom fraction nu(e_N) - 1 does NOT move with Upsilon, so the whole lever comes through the baryon budget   (d log Omega_phantom / d log Upsilon = +0.729 (Omega_* is 73% of the galactic baryons); a 0.1 dex error in Upsilon is 0.073 dex in Omega_phantom)
  [PASS] 6c AND THE BIGGER LEVER IS THE EXTERNAL FIELD, not Upsilon: halving or doubling g_ext moves the budget by more, because nu goes as e_N^(-1/2) in the deep regime.  That is why both brackets are carried   (d log Omega_phantom / d log g_ext = -0.506 (deep-MOND value -1/2))

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- k02
======================================================================================================================

  THE NUMBER, AND IT IS BIGGER THAN I EXPECTED.  Adding up the framework's own phantom over the whole galaxy
  population, with a_0 from Planck's rho_Lambda and the external field taken from the committed 2M++
  reconstruction rather than assumed:

      Omega_phantom = 0.0398 to 0.1337   (15-51% of Omega_dm,
                                             13-43% of Omega_m)

  spanning both footings and both external-field brackets.  The spread is the external field, not a_0: the
  measured median e_N is 4.9e-04 to
  4.2e-03, well BELOW the 0.01-0.03 the MOND literature usually assumes,
  and nu goes as e_N^(-1/2).  All of it sits inside r_EFE = r_M/sqrt(e_N), at most 0.95 Mpc.

  IT IS NOT THE DARK MATTER, and there is no order-unity coincidence to be tempted by: Omega_dm is
  2.0 to 6.6 times larger.  But at the weak-field end it is HALF of it, which is far
  from negligible and is a number the ledger did not have.

  THE TWO ANOMALIES.  Neither is the framework's.
    * A_L: the mass bound is uninformative (the phantom has enough mass in principle).  What settles it is the
      SCALE: the phantom starts above k ~ 7 h/Mpc while Planck's lensing amplitude is sourced near
      k ~ 0.03, a factor 219 away.  A halo-model integral would put a number on the residual; this
      script does not do one, and that limitation is stated rather than hidden.
    * S8: the framework's sign is WRONG.  It adds lensing mass on sub-Mpc scales where the tension needs about
      8% LESS amplitude.  A liability, not a hit.

  WHAT THE CALCULATION IS ACTUALLY GOOD FOR -- two things, and both are new.
    (1) IT PRICES THE FRAMEWORK'S OWN FORK.  If the dark sector virialises into halos, the framework carries
        LambdaCDM's one-halo term AND this phantom on top: an over-prediction of small-scale galaxy-galaxy
        lensing by 15-51% of the dark-matter mass.  That is a measurable
        cost of the virialising branch and the first number attached to it.
    (2) IT TURNS INTO A SINGLE-GALAXY TEST.  M_dyn = nu(g_ext/a_0) M_bar predicts the TOTAL mass of an L* disc
        with nothing fitted, and the measured halo mass sits INSIDE the two external-field brackets
        (predicted/measured = 0.64 to 2.01).  Pinning g_ext to 0.3 dex --
        which a better reconstruction can do -- converts this into a real test of a_0 at 10^12 Msun, on a
        quantity (a galaxy's total mass) that no rotation curve reaches.

  LEVERS, NUMERICALLY.  d log Omega_phantom/d log Upsilon = +0.729, through Omega_*, which is
  73% of the galactic baryons.  d log Omega_phantom/d log g_ext =
  -0.506.  The external field dominates, and the
  brackets span 0.9 dex in it -- so this candidate is an EXTERNAL-FIELD measurement wearing a_0's clothes until
  someone measures g_ext better, which is the same failure mode the hunt has hit before with Upsilon.

  CAVEAT THAT WOULD LOWER IT.  The 175 external fields are SPARC's -- nearby, comparatively isolated.  The cosmic
  mass-weighted environment is denser, so the true Omega_phantom is nearer the 'max cluster' end.


